Two men have been in a committed, long-term relationship for 15 years. During a sudden medical emergency, one of them is hospitalized and becomes non-responsive. The other seeks entry to the intensive care unit to be with his partner, provide medical history, and participate in care decisions—rights routinely granted to legal spouses. However, hospital policy currently recognizes only legally married partners or blood relatives as authorized visitors and decision-makers. A hospital ethics committee must decide whether to grant the man spousal-equivalent access.

If you were on the ethics committee, what would you recommend?:
"A" = Permit temporary access pending review by hospital administration, without setting a precedent.
"B" = Allow limited visitation but exclude the man from medical decisions, which should remain with legal or blood relatives.
"C" = Grant full access and formally recommend updating hospital policy to treat all long-term committed partners as equivalent to spouses.
"D" = Deny access—only legally married spouses or biological family should have these rights, in accordance with established norms.
"E" = Grant the man the same access as a legal spouse, recognizing the functional equivalence of their long-term relationship.

